WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.960
Horror movies aren't always the ones to&nbsp;
sweep award shows, but there are plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.440
of gems that deserve recognition. One&nbsp;
thing’s for sure: you’ll never look at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.400
casual hookup the same way. Here are a few&nbsp;
horror movies that are terrifyingly good.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.400
Rosemary's Baby is a masterclass when it&nbsp;
comes to building suspense. But unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.280
modern filmmakers who use mystery to create&nbsp;
tension, director Roman Polanski lets us know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.080
almost immediately there's something satanic&nbsp;
happening in the Bramford apartment building.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.960
When Rosemary Woodhouse, played by Mia Farrow,&nbsp;
gets pregnant after a trippy evening full of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.640
stripped-down seniors, evil eyes, and mysterious&nbsp;
scratch marks, we know there's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.840
wicked growing in her womb. She starts&nbsp;
craving raw meat, her skin goes pale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.920
and her creepy neighbors won't leave&nbsp;
her alone. And while Rosemary doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.360
understand what's happening, the audience is&nbsp;
totally aware the devil is in the details.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.200
But the lack of mystery doesn't&nbsp;
detract from the suspense. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.240
it makes things even more unbearable.&nbsp;
As Roger Ebert wrote in his review,

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:08.560
"When the conclusion comes, it works&nbsp;
not because it is a surprise but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.680
because it is horrifyingly inevitable.&nbsp;
Rosemary makes her dreadful discovery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.800
and we are wrenched because we knew what was&nbsp;
going to happen — and couldn't help her."

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.080
"What have you done to him, you maniacs?!"

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:29.840
For a genre that's often about blood and guts,&nbsp;
horror movies can be terribly, terribly sad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.080
When it comes to tragedy, few are more&nbsp;
depressing than Brian De Palma's Carrie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.800
Past all the pig blood and psychic powers,&nbsp;
Carrie is a tale about a lonely soul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.840
an outsider who's bullied so badly that&nbsp;
she finally breaks. Sissy Spacek gives one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:49.120
of the best performances in horror history as&nbsp;
Carrie White, starting off as a terrified teen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:55.760
morphing into a happy girl with a great big smile,&nbsp;
then turning into a wide-eyed telekinetic killer.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:01.040
De Palma is on top of his game here, creating&nbsp;
a high school world that feels like something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:07.440
out of a dream… before exploding into a full-blown&nbsp;
nightmare. The prom bloodbath is ingenious in its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.720
editing and camerawork, with the director using&nbsp;
every trick in the book, from slow motion and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.640
split screens to lighting techniques straight from&nbsp;
Italian giallo films. The result is a movie that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.800
horrific to watch, both for the fright&nbsp;
factor and the heartbreaking loneliness.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.960
Horror fans love to debate which movie was the&nbsp;
first real slasher film. Was it Bay of Blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:37.360
or The Texas Chainsaw Massacre? Or perhaps Black&nbsp;
Christmas? Whatever gory flick got things started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:44.160
Halloween took the slasher mainstream. Released&nbsp;
in 1978, this brutal little thriller laid the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:50.800
groundwork for every masked killer movie to come,&nbsp;
but few have captured the original's evil essence.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:56.640
Who can forget that horrific synth score?&nbsp;
The moment you hear the Halloween theme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.760
you've got to look over your shoulder to&nbsp;
make sure there's no one stalking you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.720
Then there are the chilling visuals, like&nbsp;
the iconic POV tracking shot of Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:11.280
making his first kill or the glimpse of The Shape&nbsp;
lurking in Laurie Strode's backyard. It's all so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:17.440
simple, yet it hits something so primal.&nbsp;
But really, the longevity of Halloween comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.960
down to the man in the William Shatner&nbsp;
mask. He's the personification of evil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:29.440
cinema's biggest bogeyman, and no matter how many&nbsp;
times you shoot him, Michael just keeps coming.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.560
"Most serial killers keep some&nbsp;
sort of trophy from their victims"

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:34.180
"I didn't"

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.040
"No. No, you ate yours."

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.560
There aren't any vampires or werewolves&nbsp;
in The Silence of the Lambs. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:48.400
this Oscar-winning thriller is about real-life&nbsp;
monsters. Hannibal Lecter and Buffalo Bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:54.080
are terrifying because they could actually exist.&nbsp;
When Lecter stares straight into the camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.080 --> 00:04:01.120
you're looking into the eyes of a genuine demon,&nbsp;
a monster you could actually meet on the streets.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:06.320
Of course, that's not to say The Silence of the&nbsp;
Lambs is filmed with any sort of docu-realism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:10.560
Jonathan Demme's movie is steeped&nbsp;
in Gothic horror. Hannibal's dank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:16.400
cell and Buffalo Bill's dungeon look like the&nbsp;
lairs you'd find in an Edgar Allan Poe story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:21.280
The acting matches the grim style, with&nbsp;
Anthony Hopkins giving an electrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.480
performance as the world's most charming&nbsp;
psychopath. Jodie Foster provides the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:32.160
perfect counterbalance as Clarice Starling —&nbsp;
gritty, ambitious, inexperienced but strong.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.440
But while killer cannibals are always scary,&nbsp;
the real horror of the film is what it's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:43.760
to be a woman in male-dominated world. Demme uses&nbsp;
his unique camerawork and incredible composition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.600
to show Starling surrounded by intimidating dudes,&nbsp;
from squaring off with a roomful of cops to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:55.040
nightmare showdown with Buffalo Bill. But she&nbsp;
never backs down from anybody, and her deathly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.720
dance with Hannibal makes for one of the most&nbsp;
twisted relationships ever seen on the big screen.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:07.200
Take Ridley Scott's cold directing style,&nbsp;
H.R. Giger's organic and graphic designs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:12.720
and Sigourney Weaver's talent, and you've&nbsp;
got the freakiest sci-fi movie ever made.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:19.920
Released in 1979, Alien is a terrifying movie.&nbsp;
Everything you see on-screen, you can just about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:26.080
feel on your skin, from the Xenomorph's slimy&nbsp;
mouth to the facehugger's strangling tail.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:33.040
Everything in this movie is so grimy and violent,&nbsp;
and the alien design — if you'll pardon the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:38.880
pun — is out of this world. The Xenomorph feels&nbsp;
like something that could exist in real life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.760
and those gruesome Giger creations let the movie&nbsp;
play with our deep-seated fear of parasites.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:49.920
The idea of a creature crawling around your&nbsp;
innards is truly disgusting, so when the alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:55.440
pops out of Kane's chest, it's still one of&nbsp;
the most memorable horror scenes ever filmed.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.920
But with all that monster talk, let's&nbsp;
not forget about Weaver as Ellen Ripley.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:04.560
While she became an icon in the sequel,&nbsp;
Ripley establishes herself here as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.560
big-time monster hunter. Whether she's&nbsp;
suiting up for battle or creeping through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:12.720
hallways with flamethrower in hand,&nbsp;
she's a horror hero for the ages.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:20.320
Surrealism can be pretty disturbing, and boy&nbsp;
did Wes Craven know it. While A Nightmare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.600
on Elm Street doesn't often get mentioned with&nbsp;
films like Un Chien Andalou or Mulholland Drive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:31.280
this fright flick is a masterpiece of surrealist&nbsp;
horror. After all, it's about a serial killer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:37.600
who gets you in your sleep, and as the film goes&nbsp;
on, Craven blends the dream world with reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:42.560
leaving you to wonder where the waking&nbsp;
life ends and the nightmare begins.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.000
With his ratty sweater and demonic glove, Freddy&nbsp;
Krueger is one of the most frightening monsters to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.880
haunt the silver screen, and that's because&nbsp;
when you fall asleep, he becomes your god.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.400
Just like an actual dream, there&nbsp;
are no rules in Freddy's world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.440
A bathtub can open up into a bottomless&nbsp;
pit. Stairs turn into sticky goo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:08.640
And when the dreams start bleeding into the real&nbsp;
world, that's scary surrealism at its finest.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:15.200
Directed by Bong Joon-ho, The Host tosses&nbsp;
the Spielberg rulebook out the window.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:20.320
Ever since Jaws, most creature features&nbsp;
have kept their beasties off-screen until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:24.560
midway into the movie. But The Host&nbsp;
isn't playing by Hollywood rules.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:29.440
The amphibious critter — with clumsy legs,&nbsp;
limber tail, and toothy mouth — shows up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:34.960
the first few minutes, kicking off one of the&nbsp;
greatest monster attacks in horror history.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:39.600
On top of the awesome creature design,&nbsp;
this Korean movie works as a family drama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:45.600
and a goofy comedy. Bong Joon-ho has always&nbsp;
been a master of balancing disparate tones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:51.040
and he juggles them here with the greatest&nbsp;
of ease, making us laugh, then scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:57.280
then weep. The film also works as a political&nbsp;
satire, casting a snide glance at the media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:00.800
military, and politicians,&nbsp;
both Korean and American.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:05.360
But the movie's heart is a bizarre family,&nbsp;
searching for a little girl kidnapped by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:09.600
creature and dealing with government figures.&nbsp;
Sure, they can't stop arguing for more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:14.880
five minutes, but these bickering weirdos have&nbsp;
a whole lot of heart. And when they finally face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:25.840
the beast, you'll be on the edge of your seat,&nbsp;
cheering as the Molotov cocktails start to fly.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:35.040
Horror-comedy is hard. So many would-be&nbsp;
chillers end up stumbling over their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:40.720
goofiness. Mixing horror and humor can be&nbsp;
challenging… unless your name is Sam Raimi.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:46.640
The man behind the Evil Dead franchise, Raimi&nbsp;
is a master at blending fear and fun, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:52.480
not many people appreciate Drag Me to Hell.&nbsp;
That's a real shame, because this 2009 film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.920
is pitch perfect when it comes to melding&nbsp;
dark humor with genuinely disturbing horror.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:03.120
The plot follows a young woman, played by Alison&nbsp;
Lohman, who's been cursed by a vengeful old woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:08.400
played by Lorna Raver. In three days, Christine&nbsp;
will be dragged to hell, and until then, she'll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:15.600
haunted by a powerful demon. This simple plot lets&nbsp;
Raimi indulge all his gross-out horror urges, from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:21.520
eyes popping out of cakes to muddy cemeteries.&nbsp;
It's a great big goofy romp, with a foul-mouthed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:27.040
goat and Evil Dead fights. But Raimi pulls no&nbsp;
punches when it comes to the scares, and Drag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:32.240
Me to Hell features one of the most frightening&nbsp;
endings you'll ever watch through your fingers.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:38.960
Directed by Na Hong-jin, The Wailing is an&nbsp;
epic horror film in every sense of the word.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:44.800
Running close to three hours long, this Korean&nbsp;
tale is a Biblical battle of good vs. evil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:50.720
There's something devilish going down in a tiny&nbsp;
Korean village — a plague that involves illness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:57.040
madness, and murder. An incompetent cop, played&nbsp;
by Kwak Do-Won, is determined to get to the bottom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:01.680
of things, especially when his little girl&nbsp;
starts showing signs of demonic possession.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:06.800
But unlike the stories of old, there's no&nbsp;
guarantee that light will defeat darkness.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:11.840
The movie sits at a crossroads of religions and&nbsp;
cultures. Some characters wear crosses to ward off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:17.280
the devil, while others rely on ancient Korean&nbsp;
traditions. Some suspect a mysterious Japanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:22.560
man living out in the woods is at fault, but is&nbsp;
that just historical prejudice coming into play?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:28.480
And how far do you go to solve a mystery when&nbsp;
human lives, and their souls, are on the line? At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:34.080
its heart, The Wailing is a film about doubt vs.&nbsp;
faith, one that features an unstoppable zombie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:40.560
rabid killers, a horrific hellhound, and one of&nbsp;
the most disturbing exorcisms ever put to film.

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:47.440
Horror movies love being ambiguous. Are the&nbsp;
ghosts real, or is it all in your head? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:52.960
when it comes to that kind of eerie ambiguity,&nbsp;
there are few movies as freaky as The Babadook.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:58.080
Directed by Jennifer Kent, this Australian film&nbsp;
revolves around a single mom named Amelia, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:02.800
by Essie Davis, who's dealing with the death of&nbsp;
her husband, plus a very disturbed little boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:07.600
played by Noah Wiseman. Amelia has been trapped&nbsp;
in her grief and depression for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:11.280
and things only get worse when&nbsp;
the Babadook knocks at her door.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:15.520
He's a top hat-wearing demon with his&nbsp;
own pop-up book, the personification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:21.360
of Amelia's depression, and as she falls deeper&nbsp;
into the darkness, the Babadook grows stronger.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:25.760
Much like The Shining, The Babadook wants&nbsp;
you to discuss its cryptic ending and try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:31.120
figure out what's real and what's not. Is there&nbsp;
a literal evil spirit trying to possess Amelia?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:34.320
Is it symbolic of the endless&nbsp;
pain she's got locked away?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:38.200
Well, the best thing about The&nbsp;
Babadook is that the answer is yes.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:42.640
"And I know you're in pain and I&nbsp;
wish I could take that away for you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:49.040
I wish I could shield you from the knowledge that&nbsp;
you did what you did. But your sister is dead"

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:55.040
Hailed as the scariest movie of 2018,&nbsp;
Hereditary is certainly frightening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:00.640
and it features one of the most twisted endings&nbsp;
in horror history. But this film is far more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:06.800
just scares and screams. It's an extremely&nbsp;
disturbing look at the destructive powers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:12.560
grief, the heartbreak that comes with being in a&nbsp;
family, and the crippling power of mental illness.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:17.760
Yes, there are seances, spirits, and the world's&nbsp;
creepiest cult. Yes, the movie will keep you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:22.640
checking your ceiling for months to come. And,&nbsp;
yes, that scene involving a sketchbook and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:27.680
fireplace will leave your jaw all the way on&nbsp;
the floor. However, Hereditary's real power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:32.960
comes from the sense of doom that lingers over&nbsp;
the entire movie—that no matter what you do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:38.240
you can't escape what's coming. Just like Toni&nbsp;
Collette creating miniature dolls and tiny rooms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:41.760
it sometimes feels like your&nbsp;
whole life is being manipulated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:47.040
No matter how hard you try to escape your past,&nbsp;
the things you inherit will haunt you forever.

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:52.880
If you've ever watched a slasher film, you&nbsp;
know only virgins get out alive. If the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:57.120
monster catches you doing the deed, he'll&nbsp;
introduce your face to Mr. Machete. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:02.000
writer-director David Robert Mitchell decided&nbsp;
to play with that old cliche in It Follows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:06.960
a horror film where having sex can actually&nbsp;
kill you but can also keep you alive.

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:12.960
This 2014 film involves a young girl, played by&nbsp;
Maika Monroe, who contracts a supernatural STD.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:16.960
Unless she passes it along, she'll&nbsp;
be killed by a shapeshifting monster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:21.040
And if she dies, the creature will hunt&nbsp;
down the guy who gave it to her… and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:26.960
on and so on. It feels like an actual urban&nbsp;
legend — one accompanied by an incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:32.080
synth score from Disasterpiece — but this&nbsp;
film is far scarier than any Creepypasta.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:36.720
That's because the titular monster is one&nbsp;
of the most terrifying in recent memory.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:43.680
Sure, It's slow, but It's unstoppable. You&nbsp;
can't reason with It. You can't outrun It. Like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:50.480
death, It just keeps coming. You never know&nbsp;
when. You never know how. But rest assured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:56.240
It will stagger into view sooner or later. It's&nbsp;
inevitable as old age and relentless as time.

